% Operators, and the three fixed variant selectors of sql/MAP.md §4.3.

### name: op.arith.add
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
1 + 2
--- expect
(1 + 2)
===
### name: op.arith.nesting-is-parenthesised-by-the-templates
--- note
Every binary template wraps itself, so correctness needs no precedence table
and the output is byte-stable. Noisier than a human would write, on purpose.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
1 + 2 * 3 - 4
--- expect
((1 + (2 * 3)) - 4)
===
### name: op.arith.modulo-is-a-function-here
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
5 % 3
--- expect
MOD(5, 3)
===
### name: op.arith.negate
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
-(1 + 2)
--- expect
(-(1 + 2))
===
### name: op.concat.text
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
"a" & "b"
--- expect
CONCAT('a', 'b')
===
### name: op.logic.and-or
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
TRUE AND FALSE OR TRUE
--- expect
((TRUE AND FALSE) OR TRUE)
===
### name: op.logic.not
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
NOT TRUE
--- expect
(NOT TRUE)
===
### name: op.logic.xor
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
TRUE XOR FALSE
--- expect
(TRUE XOR FALSE)
===
### name: op.compare.num-variant-when-both-sides-are-numeric
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
1 < 2
--- expect
(1 < 2)
===
### name: op.compare.coerce-variant-when-a-side-is-not
--- note
SEL's == compares numerically after aligning scale, so "5.00" == "5" is TRUE.
A bare = between two text columns compares text and would say FALSE.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"A": {"kind": "column", "column": "a", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
A == 5
--- expect
(CAST(`a` AS DECIMAL(65,10)) = CAST(5 AS DECIMAL(65,10)))
===
### name: op.compare.coerce-variant-for-unknown-columns
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"A": {"kind": "column", "column": "a"}}
--- source
A >= 10
--- expect
(CAST(`a` AS DECIMAL(65,10)) >= CAST(10 AS DECIMAL(65,10)))
===
### name: op.compare.text-family-forces-a-binary-collation
--- note
MariaDB's default collation is case-insensitive, so a bare = would make
"A" $== "a" true in the database and false in SEL.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
"A" $== "a"
--- expect
(CAST('A' AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin = CAST('a' AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin)
===
### name: op.compare.text-family-ordering
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
"a" $< "b"
--- expect
(CAST('a' AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin < CAST('b' AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin)
===
### name: op.eql.scalars-are-a-text-compare
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
"a" EQL "a"
--- expect
(CAST('a' AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin = CAST('a' AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin)
===
### name: op.in.literal-list
--- note
Not SQL's IN. SEL's IN is EQL-based, so each element is a byte comparison, and
casting each element inside a variadic template is not expressible. Casting only
the needle is not enough either: CAST(3.0 AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin IN (3) is
1 on MariaDB 11.8, because the numeric right-hand side pulls the comparison back
to numbers. The cast has already cost the index SQL's IN would have used.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"S": {"kind": "column", "column": "state", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
S IN ("open", "held")
--- expect
((CAST(`state` AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin = CAST('open' AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin) OR (CAST(`state` AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin = CAST('held' AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin))
===
### name: op.in.scalar-right-hand-side
--- note
spec §5.4: a right operand with no children is compared directly.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
"a" IN "a"
--- expect
(CAST('a' AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin = CAST('a' AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin)
===
### name: op.bitwise.refused-with-a-reason
--- note
The reason comes from the map and reaches the caller as the message.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
TO_UTF8("a") BAND TO_UTF8("b")
--- error
E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED 1:14
